              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "The shelf life of sliced American cheese depends heavily on whether you bought freshly sliced cheese from a deli counter or pre-packaged, individually wrapped processed slices. \n\n---\n\n### **1. Freshly Sliced Deli American Cheese**\n*(Cheese sliced for you at the deli counter, or deli-brand blocks like Land O'Lakes)*\n* **In the Fridge:** Typically **2 to 3 weeks**. However, for the absolute best quality and flavor, deli brands recommend consuming it within **5 to 7 days** of purchase. \n* **In the Freezer:** Up to **8 months**. *Note: Freezing changes the physical texture of the cheese, making it crumbly. Thawed deli cheese is still safe to eat but is best reserved for melting (such as in grilled cheese, sauces, or casseroles) rather than eating cold.*\n\n### **2. Pre-Packaged, Individually Wrapped Slices**\n*(e.g., Kraft Singles or generic store-brand processed singles)*\nBecause these slices undergo a pasteurization process and contain emulsifiers and preservatives, they have a remarkably long shelf life:\n* **In the Fridge (Unopened):** Safe to eat up to the printed \"best-by\" date, and usually remains perfectly fine for **1 to 2 months** past that date.\n* **In the Fridge (Opened):** Once the outer packaging is opened, the individual slices can last **1 to 2 months** as long as their individual plastic wrappers remain sealed and intact. \n* **In the Freezer:** Up to **6 to 8 months** (though, like deli cheese, freezing will make them crumbly and harder to peel apart).\n\n---\n\n### **Signs That Sliced American Cheese Has Spoiled**\nAlways inspect your cheese before consuming, regardless of what the expiration date says:\n* **Mold:** If you see white, green, or blue mold growing on sliced cheese, **discard the entire package**. *Unlike a solid block of hard cheese, you cannot simply cut the mold off of pre-sliced cheese, as mold spores and toxins can easily spread throughout the damp spaces between slices.*\n* **Texture Changes:** If the edges of the cheese are hard, dry, darkened, or cracked, it has lost its moisture. While it may still be safe to eat, it will not taste good or melt properly.\n* **Off Smell/Taste:** If the cheese smells sour, yeast-like, or generally \"off\" (or has absorbed odors from other foods in your fridge), throw it away.\n\n### **Tips to Keep It Fresh Longer**\n* **Location matters:** Store cheese on a middle shelf or in the deli drawer. Avoid storing cheese in the refrigerator door, as temperature fluctuations from opening the door will speed up spoilage.\n* **Keep it airtight:** For deli cheese, squeeze as much air out of the plastic bag as possible before sealing it. If the original bag doesn't seal well, wrap the slices tightly in aluminum foil or place them in a zip-top freezer bag.",
